00:00:04.319 --> 00:00:06.470 align:start position:0%
 
hi my name is yunju i'm a master's  student in the systems medicine program  at georgetown  this is my final project for my machine  learning course and it's on using  unsupervised clustering to examine  multiple classes of opioid use disorder  patients  opioid use disorder is a chronic  physical and psychological dependence on  opiates leading to clinically  significant impairment or distress  biologically is characterized by a  rewiring of the brain's pleasure centers  shown here  when a cortex has received a stimulus  such as an opioid it sends a signal to  the ventral tegmental area which then  releases dopamine into the nucleus  accumbens amygdala and the prefrontal  cortex  the nucleus accumbens then activates  motor function while the prefrontal  cortex focuses attention to the  pleasurable sensations brought by the  stimulus  there are both genetic risk factors that  can make someone more susceptible to  addiction in the first place as well as  environmental factors such as  accessibility to opiates and other  stressful environmental situations have  pushed people to resort to opiate use  the prevalence of oud has significantly  increased since the early 2000s and  there is currently an oud epidemic in  the united states  with the onset of the cobit 19 pandemic  opiate use has become even more  widespread likely due to the financial  medical and social difficulties that  have been  amplified  consequently there is a huge unmet need  to better understand the impacts of oud  on the brain  for my project i utilize this gene  expression omnibus transcriptomic data  set of 80 samples consisting of 20  control nucleus accumbens samples 20  control dorsolateral prefrontal cortex  20 oud nucleus accumbens and 20 oud  dorsolateral prefrontal cortex samples  there are 60  676 genes total in this data set  these are the methods that i used first  since protein coding rna and long  non-coding rna have separate expression  profiles they were analyzed separately  so the data set was  filtered for either protein coding rna  or long non-coding rna based on gene  names from ensembl biomart for each  subset of data data was annotated with  corresponding metadata containing  clinical characteristics of each sample  such as oud diagnosis and brain region  then data exploration was conducted  using differential gene expression  analysis  and results were displayed using a  volcano plot  significant genes were filtered and  samples were first clustered using a  heat map analysis  principal component analysis was  conducted to explore the variance of  samples in the data set and pca results  were displayed  with scatter plots  unsupervised clustering with affinity  propagation was used to assess how  samples clustered together based on  significant genes  for each cluster the grouped samples  were extracted and clusters were further  explored using differential gene  expression analysis  a few clusters characterized by all or  mostly control samples  in either dorsolateral prefrontal cortex  or nucleus accumbens brain regions were  grouped together to act as a control for  clusters of oud samples with the goal of  minimizing confounding effects by brain  region  ensemble names were converted to gene  names using the my gene python package  and top significant genes were inputted  into enricher to assess pathway  involvement in select clusters  all data analysis was carried out in  python on a google collab script file  provided at the end of this presentation  that can be readily run to generate the  results from this project  for protein coding rna differential gene  expression analysis revealed 183  significantly altered genes in oud with  147 upregulated and 36 down regulated  genes  heat map analysis of the samples  revealed a cluster of oud samples  characterized by upregulation of several  significant genes  principal component analysis suggested  large amounts of noise within the oud  and control samples and samples were  grouped by brain region rather than oud  diagnosis  the pca accounted for approximately 46  percent of the variance in the data and  noise variance was 6.54  affinity propagation unsupervised  clustering of the significant data  resulted in 11 clusters  cluster 3 only contained one sample and  clusters 6 7 and 10 contained  approximately equal amounts of oud and  control samples  the remaining clusters were further  analyzed using differential expression  and pathway enrichment analysis tool in  richer  clusters one and two each contained oud  samples only in the nucleus accumbens  regions and cluster four contained  control samples only in the also only in  the nucleus of cummins region  consequently cluster four was used as a  control to compare clusters one and two  differential expression analysis using  this contrast revealed 229 significant  genes in cluster 1  and a  166 significant genes in cluster 2 with  92 of these genes overlapping between  the two clusters  strikingly clusters wanted to appear to  be characterized by significantly  different pathways based on enricher  analysis  while cluster 1 involved il-6 jack stat3  and il-2-5  and tnf-alpha and f-kappa b signaling  shown here  cluster 2  tended to involve a variety of pathways  including nicotine addiction signaling  wind signaling and the ras pathway  clusters 8 and 9 also contain only oud  samples but from  both dorsolateral prefrontal cortex and  nucleus accumbens brain regions  on the other hand clusters 4 5 and 11  contain mostly control samples  when combined so the combination of  clusters 4 5 and 11 was used as a  control to further analyze clusters 8  and 9.  based on this contrast cluster 8 had 191  significant genes  while cluster 9 had 57 and 36 of these  genes overlapped between the two  these two clusters appeared to be  involved in largely similar inflammatory  pathways including sedicon receptor  interaction  il-6 jack stat3  il-2 step 5 and tnf-alpha and if kappa b  signaling  to check for any differences  differential gene expression was also  examined between these two clusters  which resulted in 68 significantly  altered genes in cluster 9 compared to  cluster 8.  analysis of these genes using enricher  indicated pathways involved in mineral  absorption and response to metal ions as  well as some of the inflammatory  pathways found previously such as tiana  f alpha and nfc b signaling  similar methods were applied for the  subset of data containing only long  non-coding rna  for long non-coding rna there were 93  significant  significantly altered genes in oud with  63 upregulated and 30 down regulated in  lud  just as in the protein coding analysis  heat map analysis of the significant  long non-coding rna revealed general  separation of a cluster of oud samples  though it appeared to be less clear than  in the protein coding rna data set  eca showed that reduced dimensions  accounted for a very low percentage of  the variance in long encoding rna data  at only 19  with a noise variance of 9.06 and  samples again tended to be grouped by  brain region rather than oud diagnosis  unsupervised clustering of data using  affinity propagation resulted in nine  clusters  clusters two and eight contained only  one sample and cluster three contained  almost equal amounts of oud and control  samples  clusters 1 4 and 7 contained mostly oud  samples from both brain regions  whereas clusters 5 6 and 9 contain  mostly control samples when combined  consequently the combination of clusters  5 6 and 9 were used as a control to  compare clusters 1 4 and 7.  cluster 1 contained 160 significant  genes  cluster 4 contained 29 significant genes  and cluster 7 contained 22.  while there was no overlap between  clusters 4 and 7 there were 13  overlapping long non-coding rna genes  between clusters 1 and 4 and 11  overlapping between  1 and 7.  the majority of the ensemble transcript  names could not be converted to gene  names using  the mygene package  likely due to a relative lack of  annotation of long non-coding rnas in  general  many of these transcripts appeared to be  novel or uncharacterized  as enriching results were inconclusive  and did not reveal significant pathway  involvement long non-coding rna genes  were manually examined for each cluster  using the ncbi gene database  cluster 1 has significant genes such as  serena2 and  mir210hg which are involved in microrna  regulation  part 1 which is induced by androgens and  prostate adenocarcinoma  and asthma-associated npsr-1 antisense  rna 1.  cluster 2 contains lyrs which interacts  with syncrypt to regulate mrna  metabolism and transport and il-21r  antisense rna1 which is involved in  immune responses  significant genes in cluster 3 included  a few more antisense rnas such as hip 1  alpha antisense rna3 and rho xf1  antisense rna1  in summary for both  for both protein coding and long  non-coding rna data sets data  exploration using differential gene  expression heat map analysis revealed up  regulation of several genes in oud and  some separation of a portion of oud  samples compared to control  however pca showed that the samples  tended to separate by brain region  rather than oud diagnosis  additionally it revealed that  dimensionality reduction accounts for a  very low percentage of the variance  found particularly in the long  non-coding rna data set  this may be due to the fact that long  non-coding rnas are more specific to  tissue type and are usually found in  lower concentrations leading to more  noise within the long non-coding rna  dataset  affinity propagation clustering of the  samples based on significant genes  resulted in 11 clusters when samples  were characterized by protein-coding rna  cluster 2 was particularly interesting  as it featured characteristics different  from the inflammation related pathways  present in other clusters despite the  fact that all oud samples were from  subjects who had been diagnosed with oud  for at least five years  this suggests that different oed  patients may respond better to different  therapies and points to the importance  of stratifying oud patients  in this project cluster 2 was  characterized by pathways involving camp  wind cholinergic signaling and uv  response  though it is unknown whether these  results are biologically relevant to  opiate addiction or are related to  additional clinical or environmental  factors such as substances lifestyles or  comorbidities they may provide insight  since oud is a multifactorial disease  further research should be done to  better understand the significance of  these mechanisms in oud  on the other hand long non-coding rna  filtered samples were clustered into  nine groups  three clusters contained mostly oud  samples  these clusters seem to feature micro rna  immune reproductive and epigenetic  related long non-coding rna genes but  enriching results remained inconclusive  due to a large portion of long  non-coding rnas with unknown function  and significance  however  some long non-coding rnas were among the  significant transcripts altered in oud  in the overall data set  and there there's increasing evidence  that long encoding rnas are important in  disease pathophysiology  an important future step in oud research  may be to functionally characterize a  long non-coding rna significantly  differentially altered in  oud  there were several limitations in the in  this project  there were multiple confounding factors  that this study did not account for  such as varying combinations and amounts  of subjects and  sub substances and subjects and systems  at the time of death or the presence of  other clinical disorders or  characteristics  this could have contributed for the  noise and variance within the dataset  despite the separate analysis  of protein coding and long non-coding  rna  additionally this project only utilized  one transcriptomic dataset  as oud is a multifactorial disease that  is significantly influenced by  environmental and epigenetic factors  this project provides a limited view  into the biological mechanism of oed  looking more comprehensively at other  data types such as dna methylation and  micro rna data might be useful as next  steps in understanding oud mechanisms  but overall the results in this analysis  suggest that inflammatory pathways are  enriched in oud with a subset of oud  patients experiencing other neurological  changes that may require different  interventions  chronic substance abuse is known to  activate various inflammatory processes  that are associated with neuropathology  beyond the direct action of the  substance on neurotransmitter signaling  these findings may support the use of  administering  anti-inflammatory therapies to alleviate  the cognitive and behavioral  manifestations of opiate addiction  and for patients that may not respond to  such therapies as is the case in many  clinical trials additional clinical  transcriptional genomic and epigenetic  data as well as increased  characterization of long non-coding rnas  may be valuable in providing more  precise treatment  these were my references  this eighth one is a publication that  originally generated and analyzed the  data set used in this project  thank you for listening i'd like to  thank mr brotzky for instructing our  machine learning course and for teaching  us all the concepts used in my project  in supporting our class  as well as pine biotech in the omixlogic  program  i'd also like to thank dr vasudevan the  program director for our systems  medicine program for being such a  supportive advisor  and finally  also my machine learning class  i've also included the link to my collab  python code here which contains all the  code for my project  thank you

